From: Lars Karlslund <lars@karlslund.dk>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: hamoa: restore CL5 for system sleep only

Jens dropped cluster_cl5 from the CPU clusters (6a545a4a23d5, suggested
by Marc Zyngier) because CL5 from runtime idle resets X1 SoCs. But the
DRIPS system state 0x0200c354 is composed on top of CL5 (0x54): with the
clusters limited to CL4 the firmware rejected every DRIPS request in
s2idle (115 million rejections over 90 minutes on this T14s at EL2).

Restore CL5 and mark it system-sleep-only, as the Surface Pro 11 EL2 setup
does, so runtime idle still stops at CL4. Needs the DC ZVA erratum
workaround.
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
index 6a4c704f58dd..ac431a79f5aa 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
@@ -303,6 +303,12 @@ cluster_cl5: cluster-sleep-1 {
 				entry-latency-us = <2000>;
 				exit-latency-us = <2000>;
 				min-residency-us = <7000>;
+				/*
+				 * CL5 from runtime idle resets X1 (DC ZVA erratum), but
+				 * DRIPS (0x0200c354) is built on it: allow it for system
+				 * sleep only.
+				 */
+				system-sleep-only;
 			};
 
 			domain_ss3: domain-sleep-0 {
@@ -454,19 +460,19 @@ cpu_pd11: power-domain-cpu11 {
 
 		cluster_pd0: power-domain-cpu-cluster0 {
 			#power-domain-cells = <0>;
-			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_cl4>;
+			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_cl4>, <&cluster_cl5>;
 			power-domains = <&system_pd>;
 		};
 
 		cluster_pd1: power-domain-cpu-cluster1 {
 			#power-domain-cells = <0>;
-			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_cl4>;
+			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_cl4>, <&cluster_cl5>;
 			power-domains = <&system_pd>;
 		};
 
 		cluster_pd2: power-domain-cpu-cluster2 {
 			#power-domain-cells = <0>;
-			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_cl4>;
+			domain-idle-states = <&cluster_cl4>, <&cluster_cl5>;
 			power-domains = <&system_pd>;
 		};
 
